### 1.4 Derived on-disk / in-memory layout (the numbers you need)

Sector = **512 B** (`tools/createimage.c` aligns to 512; SD block id == 512-byte sector). Block = **4 KiB = 8 sectors**.

| Region | Sectors | Count | Capacity |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kernel image | 0 – 2047 | 2048 | — |
| Swap | 2048 – 4095 | 2048 | `SWAP_BEGIN 2048` in `mm.h` |
| Superblock | 4096 | 1 | 512 B |
| Block map | 4097 – 4160 | 64 | 32 KiB = **262 144 bits → 262 144 blocks = 1 GiB** |
| Inode map | 4161 | 1 | 512 B = **4096 bits → 4096 inodes** |
| Inode table | 4162 – 4673 | 512 | 512×8 = **4096 inodes** |
| (gap) | 4674 – 5119 | 446 | unused |
| Data blocks | 5120 – 2 102 271 | 2 097 152 | 262 144 blocks × 8 sectors |

Block `N` lives at sector `BLOCK_BEGIN_ID + 8*N`; inode `I` lives at sector `INODE_BEGIN_ID + I/8`, byte offset `64*(I%8)`.

Cache region (kernel virtual addresses; `pa = kva - KERNEL_MEM_OFFSET`):

| Symbol | KVA | Size | Sectors |
|---|---|---|---|
| `SUPERBLOCK_CACHE` | `0xffffffc05f000000` | 0x200 (512 B) | 1 |
| `INODEMAP_CACHE` | `0xffffffc05f000200` | 0x200 (512 B) | 1 |
| `BLOCKMAP_CACHE` | `0xffffffc05f000400` | 0x8000 (32 KiB) | 64 |
| `INODE_CACHE` | `0xffffffc05f008400` | 0x200 (512 B) | 1 (8 inodes) |
| `BLOCK_CACHE` | `0xffffffc05f008600` | 0x1000 (4 KiB) | 8 (1 block) |
| `FS_CACHE_END` | `0xffffffc05f009600` | — | — |

`include/os/mm.h` documents `0x5f00_0000 ~ 0x6000_0000  FS Cache` (physical), and `PGDIR_END 0x5f000000` / `MEM_END 0x60000000` — so this region is outside both the page-table allocator and the user-page allocator. No allocation needed; just write through the fixed pointers.

**Critical cache aliasing gotcha:** there is exactly **one** 4 KiB `BLOCK_CACHE` and **one** 512 B `INODE_CACHE`. Reading an indirect block clobbers the data block; reading a second inode clobbers the first. `write_inode(id)` writes the whole 512 B `INODE_CACHE` to the sector of `id` — if the cache currently holds a different sector's 8 inodes you will corrupt 7 other inodes. `cached_inode_id` / `cached_block_id` exist so you can check before re-reading. Same for `write_block`.

