    sys_move_cursor(1, 1);

    // write 'hello world!' * 10
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        sys_fwrite(fd, "hello world!\n", 13);
    }

    // read
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        sys_fread(fd, buff, 13);
        for (j = 0; j < 13; j++)
        {
            printf("%c", buff[j]);
        }
    }

    sys_fclose(fd);
}
```

Behavioural requirements this imposes:
- `fd` is used unchecked → `do_fopen` must return a usable fd for `"1.txt"`. The intended flow (README) is `touch 1.txt` in the shell first; the safe implementation is: if the file does not exist, **create it** (do not return −1, which would make `fd_list[-1]` a wild write in `do_fwrite`/`do_fclose`).
- 10 × `fwrite(13)` = 130 bytes, sequential — `wr_pos` advances across calls.
- `fread` starts from `rd_pos == 0` (independent from `wr_pos`), 10 × 13 bytes, and must return the same bytes. Expected screen output: `hello world!` ten times, starting at row 1.
- If the file already existed from a previous run, output must still be 10 clean lines → `do_fopen` should reset `rd_pos = wr_pos = 0` on open (and either truncate `size` or leave it; the read only covers the freshly-written 130 bytes so either works).

### 4.2 `/workspace/Project6-File_System/test/test_large.c` (verbatim, entire file)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/syscall.h>

#include <os.h>

char* buff = 0x20000;

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    int i;
    int size = 1;
    if(argc > 1) {
        size = atol(argv[1]);
    }
    sys_move_cursor(1, 1);

    char msg[] = "2018K8009929048-wujunliang-OS-Project6-FileSystem-TestLargeFile-YouCanControlSizeOfDataByShellInput\0"; 
    buff[0] = 0;

    int fd = sys_fopen("2.txt", O_RDWR);
    for (i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        memcpy(buff + 100 * i, msg, 100);
    }
    printf("Write Start\n");
    sys_fwrite(fd, buff, 100 * size);
    printf("Totally Write %d * 100 Byte Data\n", size);

    // read
    printf("Read Start\n");
    memset(buff, 0, size * 100);
    sys_fread(fd, buff, 100 * size);
    for(i = 0; i < size; i++){
        if(strcmp(buff + 100 * i, msg)){
            printf("Error: Num %d Time Read\n", i);
            break;
        }
    }
    if(i == size){
        printf("Test Pass!\n");
    }

    sys_fclose(fd);
}
```

This is the indirect-block test: a single `do_fwrite` of `100 * size` bytes (`size` from the shell, e.g. `exec test_large 1000` = 100 000 bytes > 10 direct blocks = 40 960 B), followed by a single `do_fread` of the same length from `rd_pos = 0`. Expected output: `Write Start` / `Totally Write N * 100 Byte Data` / `Read Start` / `Test Pass!`.

Capacity you must support: 10 direct blocks (40 KiB) + `indirect_blocks[2]`. A single-indirect block holds 4096/4 = **1024** block ids → 4 MiB each, so 10 direct + 2 single-indirect = **40 KiB + 8 MiB**. (Alternatively treat `indirect_blocks[1]` as double-indirect; single-indirect for both is enough for the test and is what the README's "要注意间址块的情况" implies.)

Watch the cache aliasing here: streaming through an indirect block means `read_block(indirect_id)` → copy the id out → `read_block(data_id)` clobbers it. Copy the needed ids to a local before touching the data block, or re-read the indirect block per data block.

