void bzero(void *dest, uint32_t len);
int strlen(const char *src);
int strcmp(const char *str1, const char *str2);
char *strcpy(char *dest, const char *src);
char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src);
int atoi(char* src);
void itoa(char *dest, int src);
```

(`atoi` here loops until `'\n'`, not `'\0'` — do not use it on NUL-terminated path strings.)

Kernel printing — `include/os/stdio.h`:

```c
int printk(const char *fmt, ...);      /* → port_write, serial only */
int vprintk(const char *fmt, va_list va);
int prints(const char *fmt, ...);      /* → screen_write, screen buffer */
int vprints(const char *fmt, va_list va);
```

`libs/printk.c`'s `mini_vsnprintf` supports `%d %u %x %X %c %s`, an `l` length modifier and `%0N` zero-padding, with a **256-byte** internal buffer per call. `prints` writes into the 80×50 `new_screen` buffer at `screen_cursor_{x,y}[cpu_id]`; the physical refresh happens on the timer tick (`reset_irq_timer()` → `screen_reflush()` in `kernel/irq/irq.c:125-132`).

`drivers/screen.c` `screen_write()` (called by `prints`) begins with `enable_sum()` and ends with `disable_sum()` — SUM is *off* after any `prints`.

Screen helpers (need `#include <screen.h>`): `void screen_scroll(int line1, int line2);`, `void screen_move_cursor(int x, int y);`, `void screen_clear(int line1, int line2);`.

`assert(cond)` from `include/assert.h` (already included by `file.c`) panics with `printk` + infinite loop.

